  "solutions": [
    {
      "kind": "BRUTE_FORCE",
      "language": "JAVASCRIPT",
      "title": "Try every subset",
      "intuition": "Check every set of houses and keep the best one with no adjacent pair.",
      "approach": "Recursively decide each house: skip it, or take it if neither neighbor (with wrap-around) is taken.",
      "pseudocode": "go(i, taken): if i == n: return 0 if not (taken[0] and taken[n-1] and n > 1) else -inf\n  best = go(i+1); if i == 0 or not taken[i-1]: best = max(best, h[i] + go(i+1) with i taken)",
      "code": "function quietHeist(houses) {\n  const n = houses.length;\n  if (n === 1) return houses[0];\n  const go = (i, prevTaken, firstTaken) => {\n    if (i === n) return 0;\n    let best = go(i + 1, false, firstTaken);\n    const blocked = prevTaken || (i === n - 1 && firstTaken);\n    if (!blocked) best = Math.max(best, houses[i] + go(i + 1, true, firstTaken || i === 0));\n    return best;\n  };\n  return go(0, false, false);\n}",
      "timeComplexity": "O(2^n)",
      "spaceComplexity": "O(n) recursion",
      "commonMistakes": [
        "Ignoring the wrap-around between the first and last houses."
      ],
      "interviewExplanation": "Branching on take or skip covers every valid selection, but without memoization it's exponential.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "title": "Step 1",
          "content": "[2,3,2]: taking house 0 blocks houses 1 and 2 → 2."
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "title": "Step 2",
          "content": "Skipping house 0 and taking house 1 → 3, the best."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "OPTIMAL",
      "language": "JAVASCRIPT",
      "title": "Two linear passes",
      "intuition": "The circle constraint means the optimal plan skips house 0 or skips house n - 1, and each of those is the line version.",
      "approach": "Write best(from, to) with two rolling values (take, skip). Return max(best(0, n - 2), best(1, n - 1)), or houses[0] when n = 1.",
      "pseudocode": "line(a..b): take = skip = 0; for i: (take, skip) = (skip + h[i], max(take, skip)); return max(take, skip)\nreturn n == 1 ? h[0] : max(line(0..n-2), line(1..n-1))",
      "code": "function quietHeist(houses) {\n  const n = houses.length;\n  if (n === 1) return houses[0];\n  const line = (from, to) => {\n    let take = 0, skip = 0;\n    for (let i = from; i <= to; i++) {\n      const next = skip + houses[i];\n      skip = Math.max(take, skip);\n      take = next;\n    }\n    return Math.max(take, skip);\n  };\n  return Math.max(line(0, n - 2), line(1, n - 1));\n}",
      "timeComplexity": "O(n)",
      "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
      "commonMistakes": [
        "Running the line version on the whole circle.",
        "Updating skip before computing take, which reads the wrong previous state."
      ],
      "interviewExplanation": "On a line I'd keep two states: the best total if I take this house, and if I skip it. The circle forbids using both the first and the last house, so the answer is the better of the line answers excluding the first house and excluding the last house. Two O(n) passes with O(1) memory.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "title": "Step 1",
          "content": "[1,2,3,1]: line(0..2) = max(1 + 3, 2) = 4."
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "title": "Step 2",
          "content": "line(1..3) = max(2 + 1, 3) = 3. The answer is 4."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
